Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 15:14:37 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 43/71] Shrink noisy tool output where it is produced, not in
 history
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The shrinker never ran. Two independent reasons, and the first hid the second.

It shrank history, which rewrites a prompt the cache has already seen, so it
was gated behind `prefix_break`: it could only shrink from an index the cache
was already invalidated from, never buy its own break. That gate is correct
arithmetic and almost never open — in 345 measured requests `prefix_break` was
set 69 times, essentially all straight after a compaction, where there is
nothing left to shrink.

And `command_is_shrinkable` read `arguments["command"]` while `exec_command`
sends `arguments["cmd"]`, so the allowlist matched nothing on any session
whatever the gate did. The `shrinkable_before_break` counter therefore reported
zero for the wrong reason.

Shrinking now happens as the tool returns, in
`ExecCommandToolOutput::to_response_item`. The short text is the only version
the prefix ever holds, so there is no cache to invalidate and the trade
disappears: the saving is free rather than something that has to clear a
re-prefill. The conditions are unchanged — allowlisted command, clean exit,
long enough to be worth it — and a failure still keeps its output whole. The
marker now names the size it removed so a rollout can be priced without the
sidecar.

Measured on a scripted nine-commit git import of a 6302-file tree, same prompt,
25 requests either way: 16,745 tokens never entered a prompt and 162,418 tokens
of carry disappeared, taking input from 629,816 to 419,820.

